Surrogate models replace a compositional steady-state pipe-flow simulator where thousands of evaluations are needed, in optimisation, uncertainty quantification or real-time use. This second part of the work builds such surrogates on the differentiable simulator of the first part, whose vectorised march generates 300 converged training samples per pipe segment in about one minute. Multilayer perceptrons trained with Adam on inlet pressure, inlet temperature and mass flow rate reproduce the outlet pressure and temperature of the well, flowline and riser of an offshore production system with a coefficient of determination above 0.997; a grid of 135 networks per segment is trained in minutes, and the three surrogates chained in series stay within 0.3\,\% in pressure and 0.3 K in temperature of the physical model while evaluating in about 15 microseconds, five orders of magnitude faster than the compiled simulator. The axial position is then added as an input so that a network returns the whole pressure and temperature profiles, with the inlet condition imposed by construction, and a physics-informed variant adds to the loss the residual of the governing equations at collocation points that are never simulated. The residual is built from the simulator's own flux formulation, scaled by the typical gradients, and its gradient with respect to the network parameters is obtained without back-propagating through the iterative flash. With one tenth of the training profiles, the residual term halves to quarters the test error and brings the violation of the conservation equations below that of the network trained on all the data. Learning curves, cross-validation, bias and three test-set strategies quantify overfitting: a split along the axial position underestimates the generalisation error by one to two orders of magnitude, and a block split exposes a bias that random splits hide.
